Hi Karol,

Apologies for the delayed response. Here are some thoughts:

Both the spMsPGOcc and sfMsPGOcc models estimate species-specific effects as random effects. With only four species, you will be estimating random effects with just four levels, which is a challenging estimation task. This can lead often lead to substantial uncertainty in model estimates, and so single-species models may actually be better for your specific scenario. With that said, if you do fit a multi-species model I would recommend using the spatial factor model (sfMsPGOcc) with likely only a single factor.
There is not one size fits all number here, as the ability to estimate covariates also depends on where the detections occur relative to the values of the covariate. Four seems like a reasonable upper bound, but really the best way to test this would be to run some simulations. Regardless of what you choose, I would focus on including covariates that you explicitly hypothesize have some effect on occupancy of the different species, and don't just try to include as many covariates as the data would allow.
Hope that helps,

jeff

