Thank you Jeff!

After running a few models though I have found that the same model run multiple times will sometimes converge really well and other times not converge and seems like some chains wander off. For the models where this occurs, the species are quite rare and there aren't many observations or years of data. I believe that specifying initial values may be something that could help with the models converging more consistently. The coefficient that is mixing the slowest is the occurrence intercept, while occurrence covariates have much less trouble, and the detection portion of the model seems to not struggle at all. I find that the highest Rhat and lowest ESS values are for the occurrence variances portion. So I was thinking of starting with only specifying initial values for tau.sq.beta. I have a question though about how the initial values are treated, and specifically in regards to the 'fix' argument. In the documentation I read: "the tag fix can be set to TRUE to fix the starting values across all chains. If fix is not specified (the default), starting values are varied randomly across chains". So if I were to specify inits such as 'tau.sq.beta = c(20, 1, 1, 1, 1)' and have 'fix = FALSE', will this make all the initial values random except for tau.sq.beta? Or will it also randomize the initial values of tau.sq.beta? And if it does, are they fully random from the priors, ignoring the initial values I specify or does it just slightly jitter these values? If it fully randomizes the initial values for everything, is there a way to randomize all except for the ones defined for tau.sq.beta? Additionally, would you expect specifying inits to help convergence vs constraining priors? I was assuming that the inits may be the way to go since the model does converge at least 50% of the time, with the only changes I assume being due to the random initial values since I haven't specified those yet. I appreciate your insight on this, and if I should provide some model summaries or more details for this, I can share those as needed.

Thank you!
Miguel

On Tuesday, October 13, 2026 at 9:25:27 PM UTC-4 jwd...@ncsu.edu wrote:
> Hi Miguel,
>
> Based on what you said, I think a standard (1 | site) random effect is likely suitable for your purposes, although it depends on a variety of factors. WAIC is useful, but its not surprising that you see fairly close WAIC values between these different models given the relatively small sample size in terms of locations for spatial models. If your goal is to solely estimate occupancy at the specific camera trap locations for the different species and you aren't doing prediction at unobserved locations, your estimates between the NNGP model and the (1 | site) model will likely be very similar. So, in practice, while one model might have slightly better WAIC than another, your conclusions would likely be effectively identical, in which case the simpler (1 | site) model would preferred. You should also look at the estimated effective spatial range of the estimated spatial factors. If they all are very small, this indicates there is not much information that can be leveraged across space to improve predictions at unobserved locations, which then minimizes the benefits of the NNGP. If the estimated effective spatial ranges are sufficiently large relative to the distribution of sites in your study area, then that is when the NNGP could prove useful in generating predictions of occupancy probability at new sites. Again, if you're not predicting at new sites, the (1 | site) effect will likely be sufficient. There is not necessarily one right approach, but looking at the predicted occupancy values at the camera locations (which seems like your main objective) and comparing those across models may reveal that the extra complexity provided by the NNGP is not necessary.
>
> Jeff
